WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.168
Lesson five point one.

2
00:00:01.476 --> 00:00:03.732
Why the measuring came before the models,

3
00:00:03.732 --> 00:00:05.322
and how the measure was tested.

4
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.245
Before any drum model was trained, the

5
00:00:11.245 --> 00:00:13.211
team built the thing that would judge it.

6
00:00:13.498 --> 00:00:15.128
The project plan gave two reasons.

7
00:00:15.416 --> 00:00:17.094
First, this kind of system fails

8
00:00:17.094 --> 00:00:17.573
gradually.

9
00:00:17.861 --> 00:00:19.970
Every published live accompanist runs;

10
00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.079
what goes wrong is the music, a little at

11
00:00:22.079 --> 00:00:22.415
a time.

12
00:00:22.702 --> 00:00:24.908
Second, music has no single right answer,

13
00:00:24.908 --> 00:00:26.969
so there's no answer key to count mistakes

14
00:00:26.969 --> 00:00:27.352
against.

15
00:00:27.755 --> 00:00:30.303
A program that crashes fails like a cliff:

16
00:00:30.303 --> 00:00:31.312
you see it at once.

17
00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:33.967
A drummer that drifts just keeps playing,

18
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.879
a bit less like a drummer each time.

19
00:00:36.198 --> 00:00:38.056
Only a measurement sees that slope.

20
00:00:39.193 --> 00:00:41.227
Both point the same way: build the

21
00:00:41.227 --> 00:00:43.362
measurement first, and let tests, not

22
00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:45.243
arguments, settle the architecture.

23
00:00:45.548 --> 00:00:47.684
A metric is a rule that turns a drum part

24
00:00:47.684 --> 00:00:48.395
into a number.

25
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.581
The list of metrics was fixed on the

26
00:00:50.581 --> 00:00:52.818
twenty-first of August, as decision eight.

27
00:00:56.247 --> 00:00:57.980
The battery asks two questions, called

28
00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:58.192
axes.

29
00:00:58.446 --> 00:01:00.306
Axis one: does it sound like a drummer?

30
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.363
It needs no guitar.

31
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:03.308
The model's windows are compared with a

32
00:01:03.308 --> 00:01:05.211
reference population: two thousand four

33
00:01:05.211 --> 00:01:06.945
hundred and forty-one two-bar windows of

34
00:01:06.945 --> 00:01:08.763
real rock drummers, from the Groove MIDI

35
00:01:08.763 --> 00:01:09.102
dataset.

36
00:01:09.355 --> 00:01:11.301
Axis two: is it in conversation with the

37
00:01:11.301 --> 00:01:11.597
guitar?

38
00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:12.781
It needs no reference.

39
00:01:13.034 --> 00:01:14.726
The drums are scored against the guitar

40
00:01:14.726 --> 00:01:15.783
they were generated from.

41
00:01:16.603 --> 00:01:19.124
On axis one, every window is a point, and

42
00:01:19.124 --> 00:01:20.439
the humans make a cloud.

43
00:01:20.768 --> 00:01:22.795
A good model's cloud overlaps theirs.

44
00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:25.480
A model that plays the human average every

45
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.220
time is a single point: right on the mean,

46
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.700
and nothing like a drummer.

47
00:01:30.029 --> 00:01:32.056
Lesson five point three measures the

48
00:01:32.056 --> 00:01:32.495
overlap.

49
00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:34.686
Why never average them?

50
00:01:35.005 --> 00:01:36.386
Take two made-up drummers.

51
00:01:36.705 --> 00:01:38.882
A sounds like a real drummer but ignores

52
00:01:38.882 --> 00:01:39.467
the guitar.

53
00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:41.751
B follows the guitar but sounds like

54
00:01:41.751 --> 00:01:42.122
nobody.

55
00:01:42.441 --> 00:01:44.884
Average each, and both get a half: the

56
00:01:44.884 --> 00:01:46.956
number hides which way each one failed.

57
00:01:47.417 --> 00:01:49.428
The decision's recorded reason goes

58
00:01:49.428 --> 00:01:52.053
further: pushing fit to the player up can

59
00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:53.059
pull quality down.

60
00:01:53.394 --> 00:01:55.685
So the two axes are always reported side

61
00:01:55.685 --> 00:01:56.131
by side.

62
00:01:59.653 --> 00:02:01.907
Here's the idea this whole topic rests on.

63
00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:04.483
A metric can measure something other than

64
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:04.966
its name.

65
00:02:05.288 --> 00:02:07.596
So before a metric ranks any model, it's

66
00:02:07.596 --> 00:02:09.904
tested on drum parts whose right answer we

67
00:02:09.904 --> 00:02:10.601
already know.

68
00:02:11.314 --> 00:02:13.127
Every axis-two metric is scored on the

69
00:02:13.127 --> 00:02:14.708
same guitar under four drum parts.

70
00:02:14.987 --> 00:02:15.963
The song's own drums.

71
00:02:16.242 --> 00:02:18.241
A stranger's: another song's drums,

72
00:02:18.241 --> 00:02:20.101
competent, but never written for this

73
00:02:20.101 --> 00:02:20.426
guitar.

74
00:02:20.705 --> 00:02:23.030
A fixed loop: kick on one and three, snare

75
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:24.983
on two and four, hats on the eighths,

76
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:26.656
repeated forever, never listening.

77
00:02:26.935 --> 00:02:29.074
And the song's own bars, shuffled: the

78
00:02:29.074 --> 00:02:30.515
same habits in the wrong order.

79
00:02:30.961 --> 00:02:33.198
For each song i, take the metric on the

80
00:02:33.198 --> 00:02:35.489
own drums, minus the metric on a control.

81
00:02:35.808 --> 00:02:37.034
That difference is d i.

82
00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:39.484
A metric is adopted only if the typical

83
00:02:39.484 --> 00:02:41.668
song's own drums beat the control, and

84
00:02:41.668 --> 00:02:43.799
beat all three, with a paired test,

85
00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:46.090
Wilcoxon's, saying the lean isn't chance.

86
00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.221
Lesson five point seven covers it.

87
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.732
The first axis-two metric was NOC:

88
00:02:53.732 --> 00:02:55.169
normalised onset coincidence.

89
00:02:55.466 --> 00:02:57.497
It counts the slots where the guitar and

90
00:02:57.497 --> 00:02:59.430
the drums start a note together, and

91
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.065
subtracts what chance would give.

92
00:03:01.362 --> 00:03:03.245
Lesson five point four does the maths.

93
00:03:03.542 --> 00:03:05.524
Here it is on seven hundred and seventy

94
00:03:05.524 --> 00:03:07.209
rock songs, counted only on the

95
00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:09.141
twenty-four off-beat slots, the ones

96
00:03:09.141 --> 00:03:10.033
between the beats.

97
00:03:10.418 --> 00:03:12.517
The song's own drums: plus nought point

98
00:03:12.517 --> 00:03:13.030
nine three.

99
00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.316
A stranger's: nought point five eight.

100
00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:17.415
Random hits read zero, so the chance

101
00:03:17.415 --> 00:03:19.281
correction works; and rotating the

102
00:03:19.281 --> 00:03:21.287
stranger off the bar also reads zero, so

103
00:03:21.287 --> 00:03:22.873
its score came from bar alignment.

104
00:03:23.153 --> 00:03:25.206
But the fixed loop, which never hears the

105
00:03:25.206 --> 00:03:26.839
guitar: nought point nine six.

106
00:03:27.118 --> 00:03:28.098
Above the real drums.

107
00:03:28.486 --> 00:03:30.295
Paired song by song, own minus loop is

108
00:03:30.295 --> 00:03:31.708
zero: no difference at all.

109
00:03:31.973 --> 00:03:33.870
NOC rewards hits in conventional places,

110
00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.724
and a loop is as conventional as drumming

111
00:03:35.724 --> 00:03:37.224
gets, while real drummers spend

112
00:03:37.224 --> 00:03:38.769
coincidences on fills and silences.

113
00:03:39.034 --> 00:03:40.976
Two things survived: random drums read

114
00:03:40.976 --> 00:03:43.094
zero, so the chance correction is sound;

115
00:03:43.094 --> 00:03:44.815
and the stranger's score is real genre

116
00:03:44.815 --> 00:03:46.801
habit, since rotating it off the bar wipes

117
00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:47.110
it out.

118
00:03:47.375 --> 00:03:49.008
On the eleventh of September NOC was

119
00:03:49.008 --> 00:03:50.111
demoted to a floor check.

120
00:03:51.382 --> 00:03:53.700
Its replacement, section following, asks

121
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.864
whether the drums change where the guitar

122
00:03:55.864 --> 00:03:56.276
changes.

123
00:03:56.585 --> 00:03:58.749
Own drums: plus nought point two two.

124
00:03:59.058 --> 00:04:01.428
Stranger, loop and shuffled: all about

125
00:04:01.428 --> 00:04:01.686
zero.

126
00:04:01.995 --> 00:04:04.313
A loop never changes, so it scores exactly

127
00:04:04.313 --> 00:04:04.571
zero.

128
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:05.910
It passes all three.

129
00:04:06.219 --> 00:04:08.280
That one is lessons five point five and

130
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.053
five point six.

131
00:04:12.463 --> 00:04:14.364
Decision eight also said: every metric

132
00:04:14.364 --> 00:04:15.314
ships with unit tests.

133
00:04:15.574 --> 00:04:17.258
A unit test is a small automatic check

134
00:04:17.258 --> 00:04:18.813
that code gives a known answer on a

135
00:04:18.813 --> 00:04:19.548
hand-built input.

136
00:04:19.807 --> 00:04:20.585
Here's a real one.

137
00:04:20.844 --> 00:04:22.485
We may not know the right value for a

138
00:04:22.485 --> 00:04:24.516
pattern, but we know the right order: a

139
00:04:24.516 --> 00:04:26.244
straight rock beat must score higher on

140
00:04:26.244 --> 00:04:27.842
backbeat presence than the same hits

141
00:04:27.842 --> 00:04:28.706
scattered at random.

142
00:04:28.965 --> 00:04:31.039
It scores one; the random pattern scores a

143
00:04:31.039 --> 00:04:31.384
quarter.

144
00:04:31.801 --> 00:04:33.751
The battery shipped with a hundred and

145
00:04:33.751 --> 00:04:35.951
eighty-eight tests, and had three hundred

146
00:04:35.951 --> 00:04:37.601
and forty-one by the eleventh of

147
00:04:37.601 --> 00:04:38.101
September.

148
00:04:38.401 --> 00:04:40.701
The checking earned its keep on day one:

149
00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:42.901
it found a bug in the battery's own core,

150
00:04:42.901 --> 00:04:44.851
where a note just before time zero was

151
00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.951
written into the last window as a phantom

152
00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:47.251
snare.

153
00:04:47.551 --> 00:04:48.601
A test now guards it.

154
00:04:49.691 --> 00:04:52.048
But a passing test only says the code does

155
00:04:52.048 --> 00:04:53.582
what the specification says.

156
00:04:53.911 --> 00:04:56.103
It can't say the specification measures

157
00:04:56.103 --> 00:04:56.815
musicianship.

158
00:04:57.144 --> 00:04:58.952
That's what the controls are for.

159
00:04:59.281 --> 00:05:01.583
NOC passed every one of its unit tests,

160
00:05:01.583 --> 00:05:02.953
and a loop still tied it.

161
00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.202
So: build the measure before the models,

162
00:05:07.202 --> 00:05:09.579
keep its two questions apart, and trust a

163
00:05:09.579 --> 00:05:11.469
metric only after its controls and

164
00:05:11.469 --> 00:05:13.197
adversaries have had a go at it.

165
00:05:13.521 --> 00:05:16.114
Next: the nine numbers axis one reads from

166
00:05:16.114 --> 00:05:16.816
every window.

